import Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination
import Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity.Finset

/-!
# Axiom's Monster port (parity-centralizer closure): `θ_Ω`, minimal vectors, lattice generators

Code- and lattice-layer lemmas of Axiom Math's proof of
`CFSG.Monster.Parity.rational_parity_centralizer_equals_visible` that the earlier ports
(`Golay.lean`, `CodeCocycle.lean`, `Lattice.lean`, `LatticeQuadratic.lean`) do not contain:

* `θ_Ω` — the Parker cocycle vanishes on the all-ones word, `θ Ω f = 0` (through the grey-code
  description `θ_grey` of `θ` on sums of the grey words `g j`);
* `eight_le_wt`, `wt_B` — Golay codewords have weight `≥ 8`, the basis words weight `8`;
* `normSq`, `mem_minVecs_iff` (`u ∈ minVecs ↔ u ∈ Λ ∧ u·u = 32`), `normSq_ge` (`u·u ≥ 32` for
  nonzero `u ∈ Λ`: the Leech lattice has no roots), `classOf_eq_iff` (two minimal vectors have the
  same class mod `2Λ` iff they agree up to sign), `neg_mem_minVecs`, `minVecs_ne_zero`,
  `neg_ne_self_of_mem_minVecs`;
* `oddMin`, `minFamily₃`, `minFamily₃_subset` — the odd minimal vectors `(∓3, ±1²³)`;
* `latticeGens`, `closure_latticeGens` — `Λ` is generated by explicit norm-`32` vectors.

## Port notes
* Namespaces `CFSG.Monster.Code` ↦ `VOA.Moonshine.MonsterGriess.Code`.  Module-system syntax
  (`module`, `public import`, `@[expose]`, `public section`) dropped.  Bodies verbatim except as
  listed here.
* Only the declarations reached from the target theorem are ported (declaration-level
  reachability over the source files, then by reading the proofs).  Omitted here: the Moufang-loop
  identities of `Parker`, the hexacode `G`/`H` submodules, the weight enumerators, the
  minimal-vector families `minFamily₁`, `minFamily₂` and the count `196560` (needed only for the
  triality basis, not for the parity theorem).
* `normSq_ge` uses `sum_mul_self_emod_sixteen` from our `LatticeQuadratic.lean` (same statement
  as the source's, with the code `C` implicit there).
* The source's `LatticeCount.lean` has its own private `neg_one_pow_val`; only the
  `LatticeShort.lean` copy is ported (the two are identical statements).
* `classOf_eq_iff`: the source's `dite_eq_left` (absent in Mathlib v4.30) ↦ `dif_pos`.
* `decide +kernel` (kernel-checked, no `native_decide`): `ZMod2_six`, `sum_lam0`, `normSq_pm`,
  `normSq_B`, `normSq_lam0` (as in the source).
* Style linters (`longLine`, `show`, `cdot`) are switched off for the verbatim source text.
